<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malik Kafur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1316">1316</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malik Kafur was a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>, sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in the early fourteenth century. He led the sultanate's most spectacular campaigns into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, extracting tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> kingdom and sacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> treasury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. By 1311 nearly every major southern Indian power had submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kafur, also known as Taj al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>, was reportedly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> descent; the 14th-century chronicler Isami calls him a Mahratta. In his youth he was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> slave of a wealthy Khwaja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Khambhat</a>, and his epithet hazar-dinari, the thousand-dinar man, is said to refer to his price, though the figure is probably a metaphorical compliment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a>'s general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-khan">Nusrat Khan</a> captured him at Khambhat during the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1299, and he was converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and presented to Alauddin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. He rose quickly as a counsellor and commander, holding the rank of barbeg by 1306.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1306 he routed a Mongol army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> that had advanced to the Ravi River, with the help of commanders including Malik Tughluq. Sent against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramachandra-of-devagiri">Ramachandra of Devagiri</a>, who had stopped paying tribute, he brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a>. In January 1310 he reached the Kakatiya capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>, and after a month-long siege its ruler, Prataparudra, surrendered and agreed to pay tribute; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> diamond was said to be among the spoils. On 25 February 1311 he besieged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwarasamudra">Dwarasamudra</a>, and on 24 April he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. In 1313 he annexed Devagiri, which he governed for two years.</p><p>Recalled when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> fell ill in 1315, he held executive power as Na'ib and seems to have driven a purge of rival officers. He had the noble Alp Khan killed and Prince Khizr Khan imprisoned at Gwalior. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziauddin-barani">Ziauddin Barani</a> wrote that the sultan had fallen deeply in love with him; some scholars read this as a homosexual relationship, while the historian Banarsi Prasad Saksena holds that it was not sexual.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> died on the night of 4 January 1316, Kafur placed the six-year-old Shihabuddin on the throne as his regent, had Khizr Khan and Shadi Khan blinded and imprisoned the prince Mubarak Shah. Alauddin's former bodyguards, led by Mubashshir, killed him within about a month. Barani was severely critical of him, but the historian Abraham Eraly argues that Barani was prejudiced against his non-Turkic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> origins and his status as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>. Sultan Firuz Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> later restored his tomb, whose site is now unknown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malik Kafur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His general <b>Malik Kafur</b> led the most spectacular campaigns in the sultanate's history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The last major war was against the Delhi Sultanate: <b>Malik Kafur</b> captured Dwarasamudra in 1311, and Veera Ballala III fought on against Delhi’s governors and the Madurai Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pandyas were less able to resist the armies of the Delhi Sultanate, whose mobile cavalry under <b>Malik Kafur</b> raided Madurai in 1311 while the kingdom was divided by civil war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…service could carry them far: after Firuz Shah’s death his old slaves turned against Sultan Abu Bakr Shah and had Nasir ud-Din Muhammad Shah placed on the throne, and under the Khaljis the slave general <b>Malik Kafur</b>, born to a Hindu family, had held power himself in 1316.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Malik Kafur's</b> campaigns, the Delhi Sultanate governed the Madurai region, known as Ma'bar, through officials.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khambhat">Khambhat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.31,72.62&amp;year=1306" aria-label="Khambhat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Malik Kafur"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5990 5601h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5990 5601h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.31,72.62&amp;year=1306">What was here in 1306? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
